\f1\fs22 \'93There isn\'92t anyone smarter than me,\'94 he said to the reflection in the mirror, then fastidiously brushed back the hair he didn\'92t have. His mirror-friend bared their perfect Airborne teeth for him to inspect. He nodded with inflated approval.\
In the living room, Leviathan\'97his black-and-tan cavadoodle\'97waited by the balcony windows, his eyes wide with expectant desperation. \'93Oh, does poor Levi need to go doo-doos?\'94 he crooned. \
Levi whimpered. All too sharp in Levi\'92s doggy mind was the memory of his master\'92s retribution the last time he\'92d failed to wait. Poor Levi couldn\'92t understand the fuss; he was certain he\'92d improved the scent of the snow leopard rug on his master\'92s cigar room floor.\
\'93Who\'92s a good boy for waiting?\'94 Johnny praised as he hoiked Levi under one arm and slid open the glass door. \'93You\'92re a good boy, yes you are.\'94 Levi licked Johnny\'92s face while he loaded him into the litter harness, and waited patiently yet nervously while Johnny wound the ratchet on the boom, lifting him into the air. Johnny stared into the glare of the morning sun, checking for elephant-sized shadows looming above. Seeing none, he quickly swung Levi over the balcony railing and steadied the harness chain. Levi stopped panting, his face a picture of relief as, dangling in mid-air dozens and dozens of storeys above the ground, he: 1) cocked his leg, and 2) lifted his tail and grunted.\
\cb3 For the less than the thirty seconds it took Levi to find complete relief, Johnny smiled with wicked pleasure. Normally, the Layer would dissolve any pet litter before it passed through and splattered the ground. But this day had ascended bright and sunny; an infrequent westerly conquered the prevailing southeast trade winds, and blew much of the Layer out over the Tasman Sea. Johnny watched as shards of Levi\'92s stream and bombing raid descended. He studied the movements of ant-sized pedestrians, and grinned with satisfaction as they scattered. He imagined he saw at least one brushing excrement out of hair and off clothing.\cb1 \
The Cloudborne had learned to hate sunny days, and the Airborne loved that they hated them.\
Johnny\'92s hubris evaporated as a barrage of thick, mucousy, cellulose-rich clumps\'97the size of Levi\'92s head\'97fell from the sky. One struck the harness chain and broke apart. Levi yelped. So did Johnny, who scrambled to retrieve Levi and get him under the cover of the balcony above.\
\'93Tax you, you taxing psychopath!\'94 Johnny shouted at the sky, and soberly noted the sound of his voice would not reach the penthouse rooftop. 
\f2\i How bad is my luck?
\f1\i0  he complained to himself. As he gathered quivering, traumatised, face-licking Levi in his arms, he cursed again the day he bought the apartment. \'93Tax you, Voltworthy!\'94 he cursed again and shook his fist at the sky.\
Pretorius Voltworthy, the flamboyant princeling of suborbital delusion, presided over a space empire whose rockets failed so reliably that pre-launch aborts were described in his prospectus as \'93milestone events.\'94 Their signature booster, the Greased Possum, became globally infamous for suffering what technicians delicately called \'93launch anxiety\'94 and \'93premature immolation\'94\'97disorders so reliably catastrophic that, on one legendary afternoon, the same rocket managed to abort itself three times on the way to the launchpad, never once ascending higher than a ten-year-old with a skipping rope and a sprained ankle. If they ever launched, they would fall out of orbit within minutes, trailing fire and inflated investor expectations. Crash landings trended best, such as the time a satellite-sized vegan sausage flattened a livestock auction, killing four cattle barons and seventeen breeding bulls. Reducing payloads to little more than a packet of crisps or a commemorative fridge magnet yielded no less embarrassment. Ever buoyant, Voltworthy was known to say, \'93Failure is simply success that hasn't had its morning coffee yet\'94\'97a motto frequently painted in ablative orange on rocket fuselages, right before the whole venture made an unscheduled rendezvous with a sheep paddock in suburban Toongabbie.\
Johnny couldn\'92t resist seizing the bargain. The fools selling the apartment clearly had no idea what they possessed: an eyrie high above the Layer with an uninterrupted north-easterly aspect\'97panoramic views of that great grey blanket smothering the harbour, draping over the Cloudborne, and on clear days, doing its level best to ruin the ocean as well. Johnny looked out from the balcony on inspection day and beamed over the crushed hopes and dreams of countless Cloudborne (well, they deserved it, didn\'92t they?). It filled him with both pride and belonging.\
He belonged to this part of the city. He deserved it.\
Unable to help himself, Johnny pulled out his tablet and flicked the deposit through on the spot. He could hardly exploit the sucker vendors fast enough. Maybe even Sarah would be impressed now. He imagined telling her over a ten-course degustation with matching wines and matching, apple-polished waiters. Perhaps she\'92d be so grateful she\'92d finally agree to come home with him\'97she would, wouldn\'92t she? At last?\
Had Johnny listened to the rational voice in his head (which, under pressure, had retreated to a dark room with a headache), he might have researched proposed developments for the building and discovered Voltworthy had approval to keep six elephants in his rooftop garden, complete with six industrial-strength gantries built for swinging each pachyderm over the rail to answer nature\'92s call. A moment\'92s due diligence would have revealed one of those elephant-sized litter harnesses was set to be installed directly above his own hastily acquired apartment, which was, in truth, the only reason it was so absurdly cheap.\
Voltworthy, legend had it, was infatuated with elephants\'97though strictly the African breed, which he insisted were fitting emblems of his own presence, while the Indian variety, Voltworthy claimed, \'93look like something Rick Moranis created in 
\f2\i Honey I Blew Up the Mole Rat.
\f1\i0 \'94 The idea of keeping pet elephants seemed extravagant to some, impossible to others, but that only revealed a naive lack of imagination when it came to a billionaire\'92s appetite for self-aggrandisement. The cost of hiring zoologists, habitat designers, dieticians, wranglers, elephant psychologists, and upgrading the building\'92s lifts to move several tonnes of homesick herbivore\'97these were mere accounting line items in the ever-flexible ledgers of Voltworthy\'92s tax minimisation empire.\
Levi whimpered in Johnny\'92s arms, which Johnny took as emphatic agreement.\
Besides, Ripken Upsell had giraffes in his penthouse (a metaphor for his sanity as well as a physical reality), and Voltworthy couldn\'92t stand the thought of being bested (or busted) by anyone.\
\'93An elephant only has to shit twelve to fifteen times a day,\'94 Johnny lamented. \'93How does it always seem to coincide with your needs, Levi?\'94 He gave his cavadoodle a comforting squeeze and set him gently on the lounge. \'93Daddy has to go to work now.\'94\
As Johnny descended by lift to the Rail floor, he gnawed over Levi\'92s recent near miss, his sense of impotence grinding noisily between his teeth. He tried to focus on this latest indignity, but failed to repel memories of a still greater injustice.\
Levi was Johnny\'92s second dog since moving into the apartment. The first, a noble cavadoodle named Gigi (short for Gigantor), had suffered a fate far worse than mere elephant droppings. She had been pelted with an entire elephant.\
Rockets can explode. Self-driving cars can crash into anything at rest. And pet litter gantries for elephants can suffer from what engineers, with hindsight, call \'93unpredictable metal fatigue from supporting\'97who could have guessed?\'97the weight of elephants.\'94 Sometimes, they collapse with shrieking mechanical rage, crash from the penthouse, and intercept any pooping cavadoodle in their path. RIP Gigi. She yelped only once.\
First responders at the elephant scene quickly settled at least one threadbare debate on social media: the Layer, no matter how caustic, cannot dissolve the carcass of a full-grown elephant.\
Exhibiting the best side of his MLPD (Multiple Lack of Personality Disorder), Voltworthy didn\'92t give a tax about the elephant. It had been a gift\'97one of three presented by the African Pirate Collective, who were profoundly grateful for Voltworthy\'92s \'93philanthropic\'94 efforts peppering the Congo Basin with rockets topped not by warheads, but by unwanted electric pimp cars. Apparently, in a jungle, there\'92s nothing more terrifying  than a spontaneous EV battery fire. The war between the pirates and the Congo was, by all accounts, brief.\
