\f1\fs22 Johnny watched for the telltale shimmer across the disc\'92s surface. He saw none and declared, coldly into his headset, \'93Five-second bursts at five hundred GeV in thirty seconds. Clear the room unless you want to cook from the inside out.\'94 The lab was already sealed.\
Orion stabbed at the exit keypad, other techs pressing from behind. \'93Wait a minute, sir!\'94 He called, his professional demeanour drowning in a rising tide of panic.\
\'93Hurry, little man,\'94 Johnny replied, rocking back in his chair and tenting his fingers.\
\'93It\'92s taxing stuck!\'94 Orion shouted.\
\'93Stop the test!\'94 yelled another tech.  \
\'93Stop!\'94  \
\'93Three\'85 two\'85\'94 Johnny flicked a switch, piping in the sound of arcing electricity at full volume. Techs crashed to the floor, arms over heads, heads between knees, primal terror fully engaged.\
After several seconds, Johnny cut the thunder show. Silence hit, then a tech tipped over clutching his chest. Others whimpered.\
Johnny laughed. \'93False alarm.\'94 He released the lock. The click was received like parole.\
\'93Tax you!\'94 a tech screamed. \'93I think Saunders has had a heart attack!\'94\
The group swarmed Saunders, torn between first aid and escaping. Eventually, terror prevailed. Johnny, thoroughly unimpressed, ordered medics to collect Saunders on a stretcher.\
Later, once a fresh team rotated in and the setup was reset, Johnny paused before repeating the prank. Some dormant strain of responsibility asserted itself. He let the techs return to their posts. He sighed and finally ran the test.\
The emitter whined from a hum to a banshee squeal as gamma rays hammered the disc. After twenty cycles, the emitter shut down, robotic arms wheeled in, and techs leaned forward at their stations, scratching necks or rearranging underwear, squinting at readouts.\
\'93No measurable phenomena,\'94 a voice reported over comms.\
\'93No change,\'94 another echoed.\
\'93Stasis maintained throughout the test window.\'94\
\'93Nothing?\'94 Johnny pressed, banishing the creeping thought that the project might be a total waste. \'93Gamma radiation has no effect whatsoever?\'94 
\f2\i Don\'92t think about it, magic neocortex.
\f1\i0  \'93What else can we try?\'94 He only realised he\'92d said it aloud when everyone looked up.\
One brave soul volunteered, \'93Sir\'85 we\'92ve tried chemicals, electric current, magnetic fields, heat, cold, every type of radiation, MRI, ultrasound, PET, x-rays, neutrino showers\'97we even hit it with a hammer.\'94\
Johnny\'92s mind flicked back to his conversation with Sarah at the Elvi bar that night the PAPER agents raided. Sarah had shown him the Gyges Disc, then snatched it back. Well, I\'92ve got it now, and no one\'92s stopping me unlocking its secrets. Fuzzy splinters of the conversation with Sarah pricked his thoughts. \'93You haven\'92t tried licking it, have you?\'94\
Eyebrows rose all around. Was this a trick?\
\'93Or tossing it?\'94\
\'93Sir,\'94 Ortolan ventured. \'93We can\'92t have someone inside the bunker tossing the disc while we hit it with lethal radiation.\'94\
Johnny pondered the veracity of Ortolan\'92s assertion, and reluctantly thought of alternatives. \'93Spin it, then\'94 he said. \'93We must spin it.\'94\
A junior tech in the test room, tasked with returning the disc to its crate, listened in. Realising the project\'92s future was looking grim, he let out a defeated sigh, shrugged, and hurled the disc like a frisbee at the wall. It rebounded, it glowed, it seemed to warm the air. Then, with a ricochet, it socked him in the teeth, slashed his cheeks, and buried itself right at the back of his throat.\
It took clinicians twenty minutes to retrieve the disc. The disc emerged unscathed. The technician, not so much.\
Johnny was almost pleased. They\'92d finally learned something useful about the disc. Now they\'92d have to get kinetic on its arse.
